  <h1>Spousal Support FAQ</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Harper Quinn (HQ), Editor-in-Chief &amp; Family Law Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <h2>Who pays spousal support?</h2>
  <p>The higher-earning spouse, regardless of gender. Modern state statutes are gender-neutral — either spouse can pay or receive spousal support based on the relative incomes, earning capacities, and circumstances of both parties. The percentage of female payers has grown significantly over the past two decades as more women out-earn their spouses in marriage. Courts do not consider gender in the support analysis; they consider income, earning capacity, marriage length, and the other statutory factors in their jurisdiction.</p>

  <h2>How long does spousal support last?</h2>
  <p>Duration varies significantly by state and by marriage length. Short marriages (under 5 years) often produce little or no permanent support; the focus is on short-term rehabilitative awards helping the lower-earning spouse transition economically. Marriages of 5&ndash;10 years typically yield support lasting approximately half the marriage length. Marriages of 10&ndash;20 years often produce support lasting 60&ndash;80% of the marriage length. Marriages of 20+ years can lead to indefinite support in most states, particularly when there is a significant income gap and limited earning capacity for the lower-earning spouse. Texas is a notable exception: duration is capped at 5 years for most marriages regardless of length.</p>

  <h2>Is spousal support taxable?</h2>
  <p>The tax treatment depends on when your divorce was finalized. For divorces finalized <strong>after December 31, 2018</strong>: spousal support is <strong>not</strong> deductible by the payer and <strong>not</strong> taxable income to the recipient under federal tax law (the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act eliminated the deduction/inclusion treatment). For divorces finalized <strong>before that date</strong>: the old rules still apply — payments are deductible to the payer and taxable to the recipient — unless the agreement was subsequently modified to elect the new treatment. State tax treatment varies and may differ from federal.</p>

  <h2>Can spousal support be modified?</h2>
  <p>Yes, in most states, upon a showing of material change in circumstances. Common bases for modification: involuntary job loss or significant reduction in income; retirement at full retirement age; significant increase in the recipient&rsquo;s income or earning capacity; the recipient&rsquo;s remarriage (which typically terminates support); or onset of a disabling health condition affecting either spouse. Some divorce agreements specify that support is non-modifiable — if the agreement includes such a clause, the parties have contractually agreed to lock in the terms regardless of changed circumstances, though courts may review whether enforcement would be unconscionable.</p>

  <h2>Does remarriage end spousal support?</h2>
  <p>Usually, for the recipient&rsquo;s remarriage. Most state statutes provide that spousal support terminates automatically upon the recipient&rsquo;s remarriage — often from the date of the remarriage without a court order required. The paying spouse should nonetheless seek a formal court order confirming termination to avoid future disputes about arrears. Cohabitation with a new partner (without marriage) may reduce or terminate support in some states, but the threshold varies: some states require proof that the cohabitation creates a supportive economic relationship; others treat cohabitation more narrowly. The payer must typically petition the court to address cohabitation&rsquo;s effect on support.</p>

  <h2>What if my ex hides income?</h2>
  <p>Discovery in divorce proceedings is designed to surface hidden income. Tools available: subpoenas for tax returns, bank statements, and business records; depositions of the spouse and their accountant; inspection of business financial records for self-employed spouses; and forensic accounting analysis for high-asset or business-income cases. Most states also allow income imputation — if a court finds that a spouse is voluntarily unemployed or underemployed to avoid a higher support obligation, the court can assign an imputed income based on earning capacity, not actual income. Evidence of deliberate income reduction shortly before or during divorce proceedings is viewed skeptically by courts.</p>

  <h2>Do prenups override the formula?</h2>
  <p>Usually yes, if the prenuptial agreement is valid and properly executed. Most states enforce prenuptial agreements that waive, limit, or fix spousal support, provided the agreement meets statutory requirements: it must be in writing, signed voluntarily without duress, supported by adequate disclosure of both parties&rsquo; financial situations, and not unconscionable at the time of enforcement. Agreements executed without adequate financial disclosure, under duress, or without the opportunity to consult independent counsel are at greatest risk of challenge. Some states also refuse to enforce complete waivers of support if enforcement would leave one spouse eligible for public assistance. Have any prenuptial agreement reviewed by an attorney before relying on it.</p>

  <h2>What about child support?</h2>
  <p>Spousal support and child support are calculated separately but interact closely. Child support generally takes priority — courts calculate and order child support first, then determine what income remains available for spousal support. Some states&rsquo; formula approaches calculate both simultaneously to avoid double-counting income. When a paying spouse is obligated for both child support and spousal support, the combined obligation can significantly affect their net disposable income and in some cases trigger a review of whether either obligation should be adjusted.</p>

  <h2>Can the judge ignore the formula?</h2>
  <p>In most states, yes — particularly for permanent support amounts and duration. Formula guidelines in most jurisdictions are starting points, not binding rules, and judges are authorized to deviate with documented reasoning. This is intentional: spousal support is meant to respond to the specific circumstances of the parties, and a formula that works well for most cases may produce an unjust result in a particular situation. The consequence is that the same income and marriage length can produce different outcomes before different judges in the same jurisdiction. This judicial discretion is part of why consultation with a local family-law attorney — who knows how the judges in your specific county approach support — is so valuable.</p>

  <h2>Does a stay-at-home parent have a strong support case?</h2>
  <p>Generally yes, particularly in long marriages. Courts in most states give significant weight to non-monetary contributions to the marriage — homemaking, primary childcare, supporting the other spouse&rsquo;s career development, and the economic value of the services provided. A spouse who spent 15 years as the primary caregiver for children and supported a high-earning career at the cost of their own professional advancement has a strong claim for above-formula support in most jurisdictions. The non-monetary contribution factor is one of the most plaintiff-favorable aspects of the multi-factor analysis.</p>

